    "sources": {
      "grounding": {
        "generic": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.npr.org/2018/06/06/617435718/chinas-zte-reportedly-strikes-preliminary-deal-to-lift-u-s-ban",
            "title": "China's ZTE Reportedly Strikes Preliminary Deal To Lift U.S. Ban : NPR",
            "snippets": [
              "- Keyboard shortcuts for audio player\n\n**China's ZTE Reportedly Strikes Preliminary Deal To Lift U.S. Ban** **The cellphone maker was sanctioned by the Commerce Department for illegal sales to Iran and North Korea. However, President Trump last month said he wanted to resolve the issue to save jobs in China.**",
              "# China's ZTE Reportedly Strikes Preliminary Deal To Lift U.S. Ban\nNg Han Guan/AP\n\nChinese cellphone maker ZTE Corp. — the target of U.S. sanctions before President Trump ordered an abrupt about-face last month — has reportedly agreed to a preliminary deal to lift a U.S. Commerce Department ban on it buying from American suppliers",
              "ZTE was originally sanctioned for illegal sales to Iran and North Korea but agreed to take corrective action. When U.S. regulators found that the company had not complied with the terms of the agreement, they cut off ZTE from its U.S. parts suppliers, a move described as a \"death sentence\" by the company, which employs 70,000 people in China",
              "### As U.S.-China Trade Talks Begin, ZTE Is In The Spotlight\n### Parallels\n\nHowever, last month, Trump tweeted that \"too many jobs in China\" were being lost because of the U.S. action and that he had instructed the Commerce Department to find a solution",
              "President Xi of China, and I, are working together to give massive Chinese phone company, ZTE, a way to get back into business, fast. Too many jobs in China lost. Commerce Department has been instructed to get it done!",
              "While the Commerce Department on Tuesday said, \"no definitive agreement has been signed by both parties,\" Reuters, citing sources, says that a tentative deal has been reached:",
              "\"The preliminary deal includes a $1 billion fine against ZTE plus $400 million in escrow to cover any future violations, sources said, adding that the terms were in line with Reuters reporting on the U.S. demands on Friday.",
              "\"The Commerce Department plans to amend its 2017 settlement agreement and count the $361 million ZTE paid as a part of that, allowing the United States to claim a total penalty of as much as $1.7 billion, the sources said.\"",
              "According to the Hong Kong-based English-language daily, \"The reprimand and forfeiture of bonuses were part of the original settlement that ZTE had reached with the US government. ZTE's failure to comply with these terms triggered the suspended denial order that prohibits American companies from doing business with the Shenzhen-based company, plunging it into crisis. ZTE has also been replacing some of its top executives in a bid to make good on its pledge.\"",
              "ZTE could not be reached for comment by either Reuters or the *Post.*\n\nEarlier, the Post reported that an Indonesian property development firm with ties to Trump was given $500 million in Chinese government loans around the time of the sudden policy change on ZTE, leading some to suggest a possible quid pro quo"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/economy/us-reaches-deal-with-chinas-zte-that-includes-1-billion-fine-commerce-secretary-says/2018/06/07/ccffa4b0-6a52-11e8-9e38-24e693b38637_story.html",
            "title": "U.S. reaches deal with China’s ZTE that includes $1 billion fine, ...",
            "snippets": [
              "The agreement also includes ZTE’s acceptance of an embedded U.S. compliance team.",
              "# U.S. reaches deal with China’s ZTE that includes $1 billion fine, commerce secretary says\nAdvertisement\n\nThe U.S. has reached a deal with China’s ZTE that includes a $1 billion fine, according to Commerce Secretary Wilbur Ross.and",
              "The Trump administration has agreed to relax its punishment of Chinese telecom company ZTE, Commerce Secretary Wilbur Ross said Thursday.\n\nThe company will pay a $1 billion fine and fund a new in-house compliance team staffed by U.S. experts, Ross told CNBC.",
              "{\"headline\":\"U.S. reaches deal with China’s ZTE that includes $1 billion fine, commerce secretary says\",\"alternativeHeadline\":\"U.S. reaches deal with China’s ZTE that includes $1 billion fine, commerce secretary says\",\"dateModified\":\"2018-06-07T23:17:22.378Z\",\"datePublished\":\"2018-06-07T13:21:56.000Z\",\"description\":\"The agreement also includes ZTE’s acceptance of an embedded U.S. compliance team.\",\"author\":[{\"name\":\"David J. Lynch\"},{\"name\":\"Simon Denyer\"},{\"name\":\"Heather Long\"}],\"isPartOf\":{\"name\":\"The Washington Post\",\"productID\":\"washingtonpost.com:basic\",\"description\":\"Breaking news and analysis on politics, business, world, national news, entertainment and more. In-depth DC, Virginia, Maryland news coverage including traffic, weather, crime, education, restaurant reviews and more.\",\"sku\":\"https://subscribe.washingtonpost.com\",\"brand\":{\"name\":\"The Washington Post\"}},\"publisher\":{\"name\":\"The Washington Post\"},\"isAccessibleForFree\":false,\"hasPart\":{\"isAccessibleForFree\":false},\"keywords\":\"business, zte, wilbur ross, zte agreement, zte fine\"}"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.voanews.com/a/us-china-reach-deal-to-end-sanctions-on-telecom-giant-zte/4428497.html",
            "title": "US, China Reach Deal to End Sanctions on Telecom Giant ZTE",
            "snippets": [
              "ZTE will pay $1 billion fine and replace its management and board in exchange for buying the American components it needs for its business",
              "# US, China Reach Deal to End Sanctions on Telecom Giant ZTE\n- By Ken Bredemeier\n\nJune 07, 2018 2:05 PM update June 07, 2018 9:30 PM\n\nUS, China Reach Deal to End Sanctions on Telecom Giant ZTE\n\nShare\n\nShare\n\nFILE- A sign for the ZTE booth is seen at the Mobile World Congress, the world's largest mobile phone trade show in Barcelona, Spain, Feb.",
              "The U.S. and China have reached a deal to lift sanctions against the giant Chinese ZTE telecommunications company that hobbled the firm when the United States banned the sale of American-made components it needed for its consumer products, U.S. Commerce Secretary Wilbur Ross said Thursday. Ross told CNBC the accord will force ZTE, which employs nearly 75,000 workers, to pay a $1 billion fine for violating the U.S",
              "ban on trade with North Korea and Iran, put another $400 million in escrow, and within 30 days replace its entire management and board. Ross said going forward, the deal imposes the \"most strict\" compliance on ZTE. He said the penalties should serve as a very strong deterrent for \"other potential bad actors\" to force compliance with U.S. trade restrictions",
              "\"If they do violate it again, in addition to the billion dollars they are paying us up front, we had them put $400 million in escrow,\" he said. \"The total deal is $1.4 billion. That money will be forfeited if they violate anything ... and we still retain the power to shut them down again.\"",
              "ZTE must hire a new compliance team selected by the U.S. Commerce Department for a 10-year term. \"We are literally embedding a compliance department of our choosing into the company to monitor it going forward,\" Ross said. \"They will pay for those people, but the people will report to the new chairman.\" \"This is a pretty strict settlement,\" he added. \"The strictest and largest settlement fine that has ever been brought by the Commerce Department against any violator of export controls.\"",
              "The commerce secretary said he did not think the settlement of the ZTE dispute would have any impact on ongoing contentious trade and tariff talks between the U.S. and China, the world's two biggest economies.\n\nWashington and Beijing have threatened each other with massive new tariffs on up to $150 billion of exports from the two countries, the fallout from the U.S. demand that China buy more American goods to sharply cut last year's $375 billion Chinese trade surplus with the U.S",
              "The ZTE agreement was reached after weeks of talks between U.S. and Chinese officials. The dispute stemmed from a U.S. decision to block sales of American-made components ZTE needs to manufacture its products for seven years, until 2025. The agreement calls for a 10-year suspended ban that can be activated if ZTE commits new trade violations. Most of the world first heard of the dispute over ZTE nearly a month ago following a tweet by U.S. President Donald Trump",
              "Later Thursday, Republican Senator Marco Rubio of Florida said: \"Huawei and ZTE pose a serious threat to America's national security. ... After today's decision to give #ZTE a pass, we have introduced a bipartisan amendment to restore penalties on ZTE.\"",
              "Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer of New York said, \"There is absolutely no good reason that ZTE should get a second chance and this decision marks a 180-degree turn away from the president's promise to be tough on China. ... It's up to Congress now to act to reverse the deal.\"\n\nThey both were part of a bipartisan effort behind the amendment.\n\nUS, China Reach Deal to End Sanctions on Telecom Giant ZTE\n\nShare\n\nShare\n\nFollow us\n\nPrint",
              "{\"articleSection\":\"2024 US Election\",\"isAccessibleForFree\":true,\"headline\":\"US, China Deal Ends Sanctions on Telecom Giant ZTE\",\"inLanguage\":\"en-US\",\"keywords\":\"East Asia, Economy, 2024 US Election, ZTE\",\"author\":{\"description\":\"\",\"name\":\"Ken Bredemeier\"},\"datePublished\":\"2018-06-07 18:05:40Z\",\"dateModified\":\"2018-06-08 01:34:40Z\",\"publisher\":{\"sameAs\":[\"https://www.facebook.com/VOANews\",\"https://twitter.com/voanews\",\"https://www.youtube.com/user/VOAvideo\",\"https://www.instagram.com/voanews/\"],\"name\":\"Voice of America (VOA News)\",\"alternateName\":\"\"},\"name\":\"US, China Reach Deal to End Sanctions on Telecom Giant ZTE\"}"
